+//! Retention: what leaves the store, decided from time alone — the rule as a value, and the report
+//! of carrying it out.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.9-retention`. `lakelet/4.8-compaction` gave the store closed segments, a
+//! manifest that names them, and one operation that changes what the store is: a merge, which
+//! rewrites segments and loses no record. What nothing in this store has done yet is *remove* data.
+//! A telemetry store that only ever appends fills the disk it was given, and the decision of what
+//! leaves is a policy about time: every record carries a timestamp, so "older than the retention
+//! window" is a rule somebody can write down, review, and measure.
+//!
+//! # The claim, and its two halves
+//!
+//! "Old data is gone" is two statements, and the second is the one that costs:
+//!
+//! * **everything older than the cutoff is unreachable.** No read answers a record whose timestamp
+//!   is below the cutoff — whether the file holding it was deleted or not;
+//! * **nothing newer than the cutoff was touched.** The segments that stay are the segments that
+//!   were: same order, same byte lengths, same record counts, same timestamps. That is an
+//!   *equality* claim about the index, not a count, and it is the half a pass is judged on,
+//!   because a pass that gets its arithmetic wrong throws away data that was still an answer.
+//!
+//! # The rules this build's policy is made of
+//!
+//! * **whole segments, never part of one.** A segment expires exactly when its *last* timestamp is
+//!   below the cutoff (`SegmentInfo::last_millis`). Removing a record from inside a file means
+//!   rewriting that file, and a retention pass that rewrote would pay exactly the write
+//!   amplification `lakelet/4.5-batch-writes` and `lakelet/4.8-compaction` spent two steps
+//!   measuring — to remove bytes it is trying to stop paying for;
+//! * **a segment whose age nobody wrote down is never deleted.** `SegmentInfo::last_millis` answers
+//!   `None` for a value somebody built for a decision about *size* rather than about time
+//!   (`SegmentInfo::new`, what `lakelet/4.8-compaction` plans merges from). `None` says nobody
+//!   stated when its records happened — not that they are old — so a plan keeps such a segment, and
+//!   it is not straddling either: nobody compared it with the cutoff, and calling it straddling
+//!   would claim it reaches below a boundary it was never read against. Deleting data on a number
+//!   nobody wrote down is the one decision a retention policy may not take;
+//! * **a retained segment that reaches below the cutoff straddles, and is left alone.** It holds
+//!   records on both sides, so dropping it would delete records newer than the cutoff; rewriting
+//!   it is the merge's business, if anything's. It stays where it is and its old records stop
+//!   being answers through the *read* path instead (`Snapshot::records`), while the store's floor
+//!   — the millisecond it has promised to have forgotten — moves past them;
+//! * **the cutoff is a bucket boundary, not an instant.** [`Policy::cutoff`] snaps `now - keep`
+//!   down with `Partition`, so a pass expires in whole buckets: a boundary is the same number on
+//!   every machine (no clock, no rounding, no timezone), and a pass never leaves a partial bucket
+//!   behind. The price is coarse: data is kept for somewhere between `keep` and `keep + width`;
+//! * **zero keeps everything, and the comparison is half-open.** A policy that keeps everything
+//!   answers a cutoff of `0`, which is below every timestamp this store can hold, so a plan made
+//!   at it is empty and the store does not move. A record whose timestamp is exactly the cutoff is
+//!   *kept*: `last_millis < cutoff`, not `<=`.
+//!
+//! [`Policy::plan`] is the whole rule, and it is a value: it reads no clock, opens no file and
+//! touches nothing, so what a pass *would* do can be judged — by a test, by a page, by an operator
+//! with a store they are afraid to touch — without doing it. The store's half of the rule lives in
+//! `crate::engine::compaction`: the manifest carries the floor and `Segments::expire` carries a
+//! plan out.
+//!
+//! # What a plan does not say
+//!
+//! A plan names segments by index, and a plan is a value taken at a moment: it says nothing about
+//! what the store looks like when it is carried out. `Segments::expire` is where a plan meets the
+//! manifest it was made from.
+
+// The modules a later step adds are declared inside this block: a scaffold may only write inside a
+// `kiln:scaffold:begin`/`kiln:scaffold:end` region, which is what keeps your own edits safe
+// (docs/authoring.md §5).
+// kiln:scaffold:begin
+// `lakelet/4.9-retention`: the bucket arithmetic a cutoff is snapped to, shared with the windows
+// `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` aligns on.
+pub mod partition;
+// kiln:scaffold:end
+
+use crate::engine::compaction::SegmentInfo;
+
+/// The rule this store retains by: keep `keep_millis` of time, and count it in buckets of
+/// `width_millis`.
+///
+/// A policy is data on purpose, and both numbers are decisions somebody has to defend. This build
+/// ships [`Policy::DEFAULT`]: **keep everything**, in one-hour buckets. A store that deletes what
+/// it has not been asked to delete is the worst default a storage engine can ship — the value has
+/// to be set by somebody who meant it — while the bucket width is the part a caller can leave
+/// alone until then.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Policy {
+    /// How many milliseconds of history to keep. Zero keeps everything.
+    keep_millis: u64,
+    /// How wide one bucket of time is. Zero is treated as one millisecond.
+    width_millis: u64,
+}
+
+impl Policy {
+    /// The policy this build ships: keep everything, in one-hour buckets.
+    ///
+    /// Zero means "keep everything" rather than "keep nothing", and that is the whole shape of the
+    /// default: an unconfigured store must not delete data, and the first number a caller types is
+    /// the window they meant. One hour is the width because it is the bucket telemetry is read in —
+    /// `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` aligns its windows on it — and because a cutoff snapped to an
+    /// hour is a boundary an operator can say out loud.
+    pub const DEFAULT: Policy = Policy {
+        keep_millis: 0,
+        width_millis: 3_600_000,
+    };
+
+    /// A policy that keeps `keep_millis` of time in buckets `width_millis` wide.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new(keep_millis: u64, width_millis: u64) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            keep_millis,
+            width_millis,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// How many milliseconds of history this policy keeps.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn keep_millis(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.keep_millis
+    }
+
+    /// How wide one bucket of this policy's time is.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn width_millis(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.width_millis
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when this policy keeps everything: no cutoff, no plan, nothing the store may delete.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn keeps_everything(&self) -> bool {
+        self.keep_millis == 0
+    }
+
+    /// The cutoff this policy has at `now_millis`: everything strictly below it is older than the
+    /// window, and the number is a bucket boundary.
+    ///
+    /// `now - keep`, *snapped down* through [`partition::Partition::of`], which is the difference
+    /// between a
+    /// retention pass and an alarm: a raw `now - keep` moves with the clock, so the boundary a pass
+    /// deletes up to would depend on when it ran — the same store, the same policy and two
+    /// different cutoffs, minutes apart. A bucket boundary is a number two machines agree on, and a
+    /// pass always removes whole buckets, so a run at 00:59 and a run at 01:01 delete the same
+    /// thing.
+    ///
+    /// A policy that keeps everything answers `0` whatever `now_millis` is — and so does one whose
+    /// window reaches back past the epoch (`saturating_sub`): there is no millisecond below zero to
+    /// expire.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn cutoff(&self, now_millis: u64) -> u64 {
+        let _ = (self, now_millis);
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.9-retention")
+    }
+
+    /// Which of `segments` expires at `cutoff_millis`, which of them stays, and which of the ones
+    /// that stay reach below it.
+    ///
+    /// The cutoff is taken as given rather than computed from a clock here: a caller that has a
+    /// store *also* has a floor (`Segments::floor`), and a cutoff below the floor is not a cutoff
+    /// this store may act on — the store that called this is where that `max` belongs, once.
+    ///
+    /// The rule, in one sentence: *a segment expires exactly when its last timestamp is below the
+    /// cutoff; a segment that stays and whose first timestamp is below it is straddling.* Order is
+    /// the manifest's order, in both lists and in the indices of `straddling`, because the plan's
+    /// second half is the claim that the retained list *is* the manifest minus the expired ones.
+    ///
+    /// A segment whose range the index does not state is kept, and is not straddling: the rule is
+    /// total, and `None` means "nobody wrote down when these records happened" rather than "old".
+    /// A plan therefore never deletes a segment whose age nobody stated — and never calls one
+    /// straddling, because nothing compared it with the cutoff.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn plan(&self, segments: &[SegmentInfo], cutoff_millis: u64) -> Plan {
+        let _ = (self, segments, cutoff_millis);
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.9-retention")
+    }
+}
+
+/// What a pass would do: which segments expire, which stay, and which of those straddle the cutoff.
+///
+/// A plan is a value a caller can hold, log or refuse, and it is where the rule can be *read*
+/// without a store: nothing here touches a file, and the same list of segments gives the same plan
+/// every time. What it cannot say is whether the store still looks like this — that is
+/// `Segments::expire`'s question, and a plan whose segments are gone is not repaired, it is
+/// refused.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Plan {
+    /// The cutoff this plan was made at: a bucket boundary, and the number the answer is about.
+    cutoff: u64,
+    /// The segments that expire, in the manifest's order.
+    expired: Vec<SegmentInfo>,
+    /// The segments that stay, in the manifest's order and with the manifest's values.
+    retained: Vec<SegmentInfo>,
+    /// The indices of the retained segments that reach below the cutoff, ascending.
+    straddling: Vec<u64>,
+}
+
+impl Plan {
+    /// The cutoff this plan was made at.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn cutoff(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.cutoff
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when nothing expires: no segment reaches below the cutoff.
+    ///
+    /// The one test a caller needs before doing anything: an empty plan means the store does not
+    /// move — no manifest written, no file removed, no epoch — because a pass that wrote a manifest
+    /// saying what the store already said would be a disk write per call for nothing.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
+        self.expired.is_empty()
+    }
+
+    /// The segments that expire, in the manifest's order.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn expired(&self) -> &[SegmentInfo] {
+        &self.expired
+    }
+
+    /// The segments that stay, in the manifest's order, with the manifest's own values.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn retained(&self) -> &[SegmentInfo] {
+        &self.retained
+    }
+
+    /// The indices of the retained segments that reach below the cutoff.
+    ///
+    /// A straddler is the price of deleting whole files: it stays because part of it is newer than
+    /// the cutoff, and it is *not* rewritten, so its old records remain on the disk until a merge
+    /// takes it — they simply stop being answers (`Snapshot::records`).
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn straddling(&self) -> &[u64] {
+        &self.straddling
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes the expired segments cost together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn expired_bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.expired
+            .iter()
+            .fold(0u64, |total, info| total.saturating_add(info.bytes))
+    }
+
+    /// How many records the expired segments hold together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn expired_records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.expired
+            .iter()
+            .fold(0u64, |total, info| total.saturating_add(info.records))
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes the retained segments cost together.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn retained_bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.retained
+            .iter()
+            .fold(0u64, |total, info| total.saturating_add(info.bytes))
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one retention pass cost and what it changed, in counts.
+///
+/// The report a store's own bookkeeping is written in, and the number a page like
+/// `tests/budget_retention.rs` measures: how many segments left, what they held, and what the store
+/// holds now. Its two ends are the *conservation* the step is judged on —
+/// `segments_before - expired == segments_after` and `retained_bytes == before - reclaimed` — and
+/// the straddling count is what says out loud that some of what stayed is not an answer any more.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct ExpireReport {
+    /// The epoch the store moved to, or the one it kept when nothing expired.
+    epoch: u64,
+    /// The cutoff the pass acted at, after the floor was taken into account.
+    cutoff: u64,
+    /// How many segments the store was made of before the pass.
+    segments_before: usize,
+    /// How many it is made of after it.
+    segments_after: usize,
+    /// The segments that expired, in the manifest's order.
+    expired: Vec<SegmentInfo>,
+    /// The segments that stayed, in the manifest's order.
+    retained: Vec<SegmentInfo>,
+    /// The indices of the retained segments that reach below the cutoff.
+    straddling: Vec<u64>,
+    /// How many bytes the expired segments cost: what the store stopped holding.
+    bytes_reclaimed: u64,
+    /// How many records left with them.
+    records_expired: u64,
+}
+
+impl ExpireReport {
+    /// The report of carrying `plan` out, in the epoch the store ended at.
+    ///
+    /// Every number here is derived from the plan and the two counts, rather than passed in
+    /// separately: a report whose `bytes_reclaimed` disagreed with the segments it says expired
+    /// would be a report nobody could act on, and it is exactly the kind of disagreement a
+    /// conservation check is looking for. The store's call site is the one that matters
+    /// (`Segments::expire`); it is public so that a preview — "this plan, carried out, would say
+    /// this" — costs nothing to write.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn of(plan: &Plan, epoch: u64, segments_before: usize) -> Self {
+        let _ = (plan, epoch, segments_before);
+        todo!("step lakelet/4.9-retention")
+    }
+
+    /// The epoch the store ended at — a new one when something expired, the same one when nothing
+    /// did.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn epoch(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.epoch
+    }
+
+    /// The cutoff this pass acted at.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn cutoff(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.cutoff
+    }
+
+    /// How many segments the store was made of before the pass.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn segments_before(&self) -> usize {
+        self.segments_before
+    }
+
+    /// How many it is made of after it.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn segments_after(&self) -> usize {
+        self.segments_after
+    }
+
+    /// The segments that expired, in the manifest's order.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn expired(&self) -> &[SegmentInfo] {
+        &self.expired
+    }
+
+    /// The segments that stayed, in the manifest's order.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn retained(&self) -> &[SegmentInfo] {
+        &self.retained
+    }
+
+    /// The indices of the retained segments that reach below the cutoff.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn straddling(&self) -> &[u64] {
+        &self.straddling
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes the store stopped holding: the expired segments' bytes.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn bytes_reclaimed(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes_reclaimed
+    }
+
+    /// How many records left with them.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn records_expired(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.records_expired
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when nothing expired and the store did not move.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
+        self.expired.is_empty()
+    }
+}
